; 36-brackets.kurt

; `brackets LEFT RIGHT` declares a new bracket pair.  Kurt's own `(` `)` is
; declared exactly this way in `minimal.kurt` (`brackets ( )`), and is
; special-cased afterwards to disappear again -- so `(A)` really is just `A`.
; A *new* bracket pair you declare is not: it stays around as a genuine
; two-symbol operator, ready to carry its own meaning, e.g. absolute value
; `|x|`, or a pairing notation `⟨a, b⟩`.

brackets "[" "]"

var a
; the `[ ... ]` you declared is its own operator, shown below as `[]` --
; "the operator made of `[` and `]`", applied to `a`:
parse [a]

; Giving it meaning is up to you, exactly like for `infix`/`prefix`/
; `postfix`: declare an `arity`/`bool` signature if needed, then add `use`
; axioms about it.
